Title: "Have / Asserting"
Id: "37d38575-04ff-4929-ab90-c20d2812aba2"

Text: "A `have:` group whose second section is `asserting:` (rather than `iff:`) is an
escape hatch: it asserts that the `have:` items hold **given** the `asserting:`
items.

```mlg-fragment
[LabelHeader]?
have: <ClauseUnion>+
asserting: <ClauseUnion>+
because?: <ClauseUnion>+
by?: <Expression>+
```

```mlg-fragment
have:
. f is? \(injective)::function
asserting:
. f is? \function
because:
. f is? \function
by:
. \injectivity.implies.function
```

`because:` and `by:` are justification the checker only reference-validates; it
never proves them.

The group is accepted in three places:

- as an ordinary clause, where its `[label]` heading is optional
- as a `declares:` item, where the heading is likewise optional
- as an item of a top-level `Justification:` section, where the `[label]`
  heading is **required** and pairs with a `[:label:]` on some formulation
  elsewhere in the same group

Note the contrast with the biconditional group `have:`/`iff:`, which asserts
that its two sides are equivalent. The second section label is what
distinguishes them."
Id: "6cb4d0d8-a313-46c1-ba0a-766e7f502d3f"
